{
 "lead": "Quality factor Q measures how much energy a resonant circuit stores compared with what it loses each cycle. That one number sets the bandwidth and the selectivity: high Q gives a narrow band that picks one frequency out of many, low Q a broad, gentle response. Radio tuning, filters and receivers all rest on it.",
 "prereq": "You need: <a href=\"#series-resonance\">series resonance</a> and <a href=\"#parallel-resonance\">parallel resonance</a>, and average power P = I²R in a resistor.",
 "sections": [
  {
   "h3": "Quality factor from energy",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "At resonance, a series RLC circuit stores energy in the inductor's magnetic field and the capacitor's electric field. The current and voltage oscillate at f₀, while the energy in L and in C each swings at 2f₀, passing back and forth between the two; at resonance their total stays constant. The resistor removes energy as heat every cycle. Q compares the energy kept to the energy lost."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Q = 2π × W<sub>max</sub> / W<sub>lost</sub></b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here w<sub>max</sub> is the maximum energy stored in the circuit, in joules (J); W<sub>lost</sub> is the energy turned into heat in R in one period, in J; Q has no unit."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a series RLC at resonance, W<sub>max</sub> = ½ L I<sub>m</sub>² and W<sub>lost</sub> = (I<sub>m</sub>²/2) R T, where I<sub>m</sub> is the peak current in A and T = 1/f₀ is the period in s. Substituting gives Q = 2π f₀ L/R, the series result."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Q<sub>series</sub> = ω₀L / R = 1 / (ω₀CR)</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here ω₀ = 2πf₀ is the resonant angular frequency in rad/s; f₀ = 1/(2π√(LC)) is the resonant frequency in Hz."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a parallel RLC near resonance, the same energy definition gives a reciprocal use of R. The shunt resistor removes less energy when R is large, so Q rises with R."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>Q<sub>parallel</sub> = R / (ω₀L) = ω₀CR</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here r is the shunt resistance in ohms (Ω); L and C are the same as above."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Bandwidth and half-power frequencies",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Bandwidth BW is the frequency range between the two half-power frequencies f₁ and f₂. At these frequencies the power in R is half its maximum. In a series circuit the current is I₀/√2 ≈ 0.707 I₀; in a parallel circuit the tank voltage is V₀/√2, where V₀ is the tank voltage at resonance. The half-power points are also called the −3 dB points."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>BW = f₂ − f₁</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here f₁ is the lower half-power frequency in Hz; f₂ is the upper half-power frequency in Hz; BW is in Hz."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "At resonance a series RLC has impedance R. At a half-power point the impedance magnitude is |Z| = √(R² + (ωL − 1/(ωC))²) = R√2. Solving this condition gives the exact half-power angular frequencies."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>ω₁,₂ = ω₀(√(1 + 1/(4Q²)) ∓ 1/(2Q))</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here ω₁ is the lower half-power angular frequency; ω₂ is the upper half-power angular frequency; Q is the resonant quality factor."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "The two half-power frequencies satisfy f₁f₂ = f₀², so f₀ is their geometric mean, not their average. Their average is f₀√(1 + 1/(4Q²)), slightly above f₀. For Q ≥ 10 the difference is under 0.2 %, so the points sit almost evenly on each side:"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>f₁ ≈ f₀ − BW/2,  f₂ ≈ f₀ + BW/2</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here f₀ is the resonant frequency in Hz; BW is the bandwidth in Hz; the approximation is valid for Q ≥ 10."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Subtracting the exact pair gives the bandwidth with no approximation: ω₂ − ω₁ = ω₀/Q. For a series RLC that is R/L, so BW = R/(2πL), independent of C; for a parallel RLC it is 1/(RC)."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "<b>BW = f₀ / Q,   ω<sub>BW</sub> = ω₀ / Q</b>"
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Here f₀ and BW are in Hz; ω₀ and ω<sub>BW</sub> are in rad/s; Q is dimensionless."
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "h3": "Selectivity and the Q link",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Selectivity is how well a circuit picks the wanted frequency and rejects nearby unwanted frequencies. Some books quote it as f₀/BW, which is Q itself; others use the fractional bandwidth BW/f₀ = 1/Q. A sharp peak means a high f₀/BW and good selectivity."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Two radio stations 10 kHz apart can be separated by a filter with BW = 5 kHz. A filter with BW = 50 kHz passes both and cannot separate them. The narrower filter has the higher Q."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "High Q gives narrow bandwidth and high selectivity. Low Q gives wide bandwidth and low selectivity. Very high Q also makes the tuned frequency sensitive to component tolerance, temperature and loading, so receivers often use moderate Q with careful tuning."
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "Effect of varying Q. Keep L = 10 mH and C = 1 µF (f₀ = 1591.5 Hz) and change only R. The table and the figure show what the examiner means by the effect of Q on resonance: f₀ stays put, the bandwidth grows in step with R, and the half-power points drift apart unevenly as Q falls."
    },
    {
     "type": "table",
     "head": [
      "R (Ω)",
      "Q",
      "BW (Hz)",
      "f₁ (Hz)",
      "f₂ (Hz)"
     ],
     "rows": [
      [
       "5",
       "20",
       "79.6",
       "1552.3",
       "1631.8"
      ],
      [
       "10",
       "10",
       "159.2",
       "1514.0",
       "1673.1"
      ],
      [
       "20",
       "5",
       "318.3",
       "1440.3",
       "1758.6"
      ],
      [
       "40",
       "2.5",
       "636.6",
       "1304.8",
       "1941.4"
      ]
     ]
    },
    {
     "type": "p",
     "text": "For a series RLC the damping ratio is ζ = 1/(2Q). A high-Q circuit has small ζ and rings for many cycles after a disturbance; the ringing itself belongs to second-order transient analysis."
    }
   ]
  }
 ],
