 "examples": [
  {
   "question": "A series RLC circuit has a resonant frequency f₀ = 1 kHz and a quality factor Q = 50. Find the bandwidth, the two half-power frequencies, and the selectivity ratio. Use the high-Q approximation.",
   "steps": [
    "Write the given values: f₀ = 1 kHz = 1000 Hz, Q = 50. The approximation is allowed because Q ≥ 10.",
    "Bandwidth: BW = f₀/Q = 1000/50 = 20 Hz.",
    "Half-power spacing: BW/2 = 20/2 = 10 Hz.",
    "Lower half-power frequency: f₁ ≈ f₀ − BW/2 = 1000 − 10 = 990 Hz.",
    "Upper half-power frequency: f₂ ≈ f₀ + BW/2 = 1000 + 10 = 1010 Hz.",
    "Selectivity ratio: BW/f₀ = 20/1000 = 0.02 = 2%."
   ],
   "answer": "The bandwidth is 20 Hz, the half-power frequencies are about 990 Hz and 1010 Hz, and the selectivity ratio is 0.02 (2%).",
   "check": "f0 = 1000.0\nQ = 50.0\nBW = f0 / Q\nf1 = f0 - BW / 2\nf2 = f0 + BW / 2\nratio = BW / f0\nprint(BW)\nprint(f1)\nprint(f2)\nprint(ratio)",
   "values": [
    20.0,
    990.0,
    1010.0,
    0.02
   ],
   "output": ""
  },
  {
   "question": "A series RLC circuit has R = 10 Ω, L = 10 mH and C = 1 µF. Find the exact half-power frequencies and compare them with the f₀ ± BW/2 estimate.",
   "steps": [
    "ω₀ = 1/√(LC) = 10 000 rad/s, f₀ = 1591.55 Hz, Q = ω₀L/R = 100/10 = 10.",
    "1/(2Q) = 0.05 and √(1 + 1/(4Q²)) = √1.0025 = 1.001249.",
    "ω₁ = 10 000 × (1.001249 − 0.05) = 9512.5 rad/s, so f₁ = 1513.96 Hz.",
    "ω₂ = 10 000 × (1.001249 + 0.05) = 10 512.5 rad/s, so f₂ = 1673.11 Hz.",
    "BW = f₂ − f₁ = 159.15 Hz = f₀/Q, exactly. Check: √(f₁f₂) = √(1513.96 × 1673.11) = 1591.55 Hz = f₀.",
    "Estimate: f₀ ± BW/2 = 1511.97 Hz and 1671.13 Hz, about 2 Hz low on each side (0.13 %)."
   ],
   "answer": "f₁ = 1513.96 Hz and f₂ = 1673.11 Hz; the high-Q estimate is within 2 Hz."
  }
 ],
 "traps": [
  "Inverse relation: Higher Q means smaller BW, not larger. If BW falls by a factor of 2, Q rises by a factor of 2 at the same f₀.",
  "Unit mix-up: BW = f₀/Q gives hertz only when f₀ is in hertz. ω<sub>BW</sub> = ω₀/Q gives rad/s. Multiplying or dividing by 2π at the wrong step changes the answer.",
  "Series and parallel R: Q<sub>series</sub> = ω₀L/R falls as R rises, while Q<sub>parallel</sub> = R/(ω₀L) rises as R rises. Using the wrong formula reverses the effect of R.",
  "Approximation limit: f₁,₂ = f₀ ± BW/2 is only good for Q ≥ 10. For low Q the exact half-power frequencies are not evenly spaced around f₀.",
  "Selectivity confusion: f₀/BW = Q and BW/f₀ = 1/Q are both quoted. A fractional bandwidth of 0.02 is 2 %, not 0.02 %. State which one you use."
 ],
 "quiz": [
  {
   "level": "remember",
   "q": "Define the quality factor Q of a resonant circuit from energy.",
   "marks": 2,
   "a": "Q = 2π × (peak energy stored)/(energy lost per cycle). It is dimensionless."
  },
  {
   "level": "understand",
   "q": "Why does a higher Q give a narrower bandwidth?",
   "marks": 3,
   "a": "Q = f₀/BW, so for a fixed f₀ a larger Q means a smaller BW. The circuit loses a smaller fraction of its stored energy per cycle, so its response falls off faster away from resonance."
  },
  {
   "level": "apply",
   "q": "A series RLC circuit has f₀ = 2 kHz and Q = 40. Find BW and the approximate half-power frequencies.",
   "marks": 4,
   "a": "BW = 2000/40 = 50 Hz. BW/2 = 25 Hz, so f₁ ≈ 1975 Hz and f₂ ≈ 2025 Hz. Q ≥ 10 so the approximation is valid."
  },
